{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"agent_message","text":"The isolated failures are confined to operand ordering in four multiply-based helpers, plus `c2Mulvs` when both operands are NaNs. GCC’s SSE instructions deliberately retain the destination operand’s NaN payload, while LLVM legally reverses commutative operands. I’m encoding the C operand order with tiny scalar SSE helpers on x86_64, with ordinary Rust fallbacks for other architectures, and routing the affected vector math through them."}}
